{
 "item": "X11",
 "capturedAt": "2026-11-05",
 "question": "When does a touch drag a block, when does the browser take it, and what does a touch on a handle do?",
 "how": "a new Shape in 4 new sections of the empty not-linked page, which scrolls (3171 high, 945 shown), 1600 wide. Touch events over the debug protocol with no touch emulation, 12 moves 25 ms apart, a pen as mouse events with pointerType pen, nothing saved",
 "rule": "a touch drags like a mouse. The browser takes it, with pointercancel and nothing moved, only if the first move is past its 15px pan distance and the press was short: 200px over 12 moves (16.7 each) with a hold of 0, 30 or 60 ms is cancelled, with 100, 150, 200, 250, 300, 500, 700 or 800 ms it moves. Up to 150px over 12 moves (12.5 each) moves at a hold of 0. A touch on a handle resizes. A touch on the empty grid draws no box and is cancelled. A pen does what a mouse does",
 "corrects": "CAP-TOUCH.json read a quick drag of 125 as cancelled after 2 moves and a touch on a corner handle as resizing nothing, one read each on the demo page. Here 125 moved in 4 of 4 and a handle resized in 4 of 4. The demo page cannot be used for this any more (decision 0006), so why they differed is not known",
 "alsoSeen": "after a handle resize with a mouse or pen ending 70px right and 70px down, the block read not selected, 3 of 3 here and on one drag in CAP-LINEHANDLE.json. After the same resize by touch it stayed selected, 4 of 4. Belongs to X2, not explained",
 "notRead": "a real device. Touch on a handle at more than 15px a move",
